## Proof

**Yes, such a function exists.** We construct one explicitly.

**Construction.** Since $\mathbb{Q}$ is countable, fix an enumeration $\mathbb{Q} = \{q_1, q_2, q_3, \dots\}$ (each rational appears exactly once). Define $f:\mathbb{Q}\to\mathbb{R}$ by
$$f(q_n) = \frac{1}{n}, \qquad n=1,2,3,\dots$$

**Bounded range.** The range of $f$ is $\{1,\tfrac12,\tfrac13,\dots\}\subset (0,1]$, which is bounded.

**Discontinuity at every rational.** Fix any point $q_n\in\mathbb{Q}$; we show $f$ is discontinuous at $q_n$. Consider any sequence $(r_k)$ of rationals with $r_k\to q_n$ and $r_k\neq q_n$ for all $k$. Write $r_k = q_{m_k}$.

**Key observation:** for any fixed $N$, the set $\{q_1,\dots,q_N\}$ is finite. Since $r_k\to q_n$ and $r_k\neq q_n$, the sequence $(r_k)$ can visit each of the finitely many points $\{q_1,\dots,q_N\}\setminus\{q_n\}$ only finitely often (otherwise a subsequence would be constantly equal to some $q_j\neq q_n$, contradicting convergence to $q_n$). Hence $m_k\to\infty$, which gives
$$f(r_k)=\frac{1}{m_k}\to 0.$$

Thus every sequence converging to $q_n$ (and not eventually equal to $q_n$) has $f$-values tending to $0$. This means $\lim_{r\to q_n} f(r)=0$ (in the subspace topology on $\mathbb{Q}$). But $f(q_n)=\tfrac1n\neq 0$. Therefore $f$ is discontinuous at $q_n$.

Since $q_n$ was arbitrary, $f$ is discontinuous at **every** rational number.

**Remark on why the domain being $\mathbb{Q}$ matters.** The crucial property used is that $\mathbb{Q}$ has no isolated points (every neighborhood of a rational contains infinitely many other rationals), which forces $m_k\to\infty$ and hence $f(r_k)\to 0$. The same enumeration-based construction works on any countable metric space without isolated points.

### PROOF COMPLETE
